#   ==== 4. greatest-fixed-point type systems ====
#   EQUI-RECURSIVE TYPES AS CYCLIC VALUES. A type is Scott data with four
#   constructors: top, a base type named by a natural (0 is Nat, 1 is Bool,
#   7 and 8 are abstract types), a function type, and a record, a Scott list
#   of fields `pair label type` with natural labels. A recursive type is a
#   fixpoint.
#
#   SUBTYPING IS CHECKED WITHOUT AN ASSUMPTION SET:
#
#       s <: top
#       base k <: base k
#       sa -> sr <: ta -> tr   when ta <: sa and sr <: tr
#       record sf <: record tf when every field l : t of tf has l : s in sf
#                              with s <: t
#
#   the conjunctions evaluated left first. On cyclic types the check comes
#   back to a pair it is already checking, and runs forever when no field
#   fails first: that is the pair the coinductive (Amadio and Cardelli)
#   check would assume, and the greatest fixed point says it is a subtype.
#   So a true subtyping of recursive types has no head, read `failed` by a
#   tabled evaluation whose keys come back, and a false one answers `false`
#   when its failing field is checked before the recursive one; the field
#   orders below are chosen so.
